A||||4||Tom88gt||alarm power door lock wiring?||||5||
Z||000000||Tom88gt||06-16-2001||11:00 PM||||I'm going to install a car alarm and it has a power door lock option.  Does the Fiero have a "negative trigger", "positive trigger" or "reverse polarity" wiring?  Has anyone installed remote door locks on their Fiero?  If so, how was it wired?<P>Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000001||PBJ||06-16-2001||11:05 PM||||positive.  access door lock relay located in rhs shroud &lt;kick pannel&gt;<P>terminal A black wire for unlock<BR>terminal B lt blue wire for lock<P>hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tom88gt||06-17-2001||12:00 PM||||Thanks.  Also, what does "positive" mean?  Are the 2 wires for the door locks always hot and the switch on the door grounds the curcuit.  Or is it the resverse, a hot switch and the black and blue wires are grounds?<P>Did you have to add extra relays or did you just hook the alarm straight to the wires?<P>Thanks,<BR>Tom||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||06-17-2001||12:38 PM||||If I remember correctly when I installed mine, there are 2 wires, both normally negative. One becomes hot when the lock button is pushed, and the other becomes hot when the unlock button is pushed.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||06-17-2001||12:42 PM||||Depends on your alarm system, but most of the time you would not have to devise any relay systems for this type of door locks. The only ones that need relay's I beleive are reverse polarity, and vehicals with the Ford High voltage door locks. Your alarm box should have 2 door lock wires. One for lock, one for unlock. When you push the button the remote for it, the appopriate wire becomes hot and triggers the door locks. Me, I just wired mine straight to the door lock motors, not to the relay. I would advise you to wire it into the relay since I am not sure what brand/model of system you have, it might not put out enough voltage. ||||reg||1
